      "id": 1,
      "text": "Oak Lane got its name in 1860 after a landowner remembered an ancient oak tree that blew down.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/East_Oak_Lane%2C_Philadelphia",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Oak Lane Branch of the Free Library of Philadelphia sits in a historic 1910 building at 12th and Oak Lane, renovated 1958 and 1999.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/East_Oak_Lane%2C_Philadelphia",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "East Oak Lane was first settled in 1683 as William Penn's first neighborhood, later called Milestown in 1711.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21,
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/East_Oak_Lane%2C_Philadelphia",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The original school at 12th and Oak Lane was a one‑story building built in 1745, donated in 1761 by Joseph Armitage.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/East_Oak_Lane%2C_Philadelphia",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The neighborhood’s streets were once lined with shops built mainly in the second half of the 20th century, giving it a small‑town Main Street feel.",
